<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#broch">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Broch</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 400 BCE–100 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A broch is a type of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> drystone tower found in northern and western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, especially in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caithness">Caithness</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a>. Built mostly between about 400 BCE and 100 CE, brochs have hollow double walls containing stairs and galleries. The best preserved, at Mousa in Shetland, still stands about 13 metres high.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/broch.jpg" alt="Broch" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Colin Smith · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Broch_of_Mousa_-_geograph.org.uk_-_2079773.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
